from questions._util import q

QUESTIONS = [
    q("sec-least", "medium", "A deploy pipeline's cloud role can create the service and also administer IAM. What do you change?", "Remove the IAM permissions from the deploy role.", "Require MFA on the deploy role's IAM actions.", "Log the role's IAM calls to a separate trail.", "Restrict the role to the pipeline's IP range.", "The pipeline should be able to deploy and nothing that lets it grant itself more. MFA cannot apply to a pipeline, and logging or IP limits do not shrink the power."),
    q("sec-least", "easy", "What does least privilege mean?", "Only the permissions the task needs.", "Read access everywhere, write where needed.", "Admin access, with every action logged.", "Admin access, granted for one hour.", "Start from the calls you observed. Delete what the last month did not use. Broad access with logging or a time limit is still broad."),
    q("sec-least", "hard", "A Role can get secrets in the namespace, but the workload only reads a ConfigMap. What is the extra power?", "A compromised pod can read every secret there.", "A compromised pod can create new secrets there.", "A compromised pod can read secrets cluster-wide.", "A compromised pod can mount secrets into others.", "get covers every secret in that namespace, including ones the pod does not mount. A Role cannot reach other namespaces. Mount the one secret you need."),
    q("sec-least", "medium", "How should a break-glass admin account be handled?", "Rarely used, logged, and stored offline.", "Used by CI, so it is always tested.", "Shared in chat, so anyone can reach it.", "Kept without MFA, so it works in outages.", "The emergency path is real. It is not the daily path. Without MFA it gets used on a Tuesday."),
    q("sec-secrets", "medium", "When is a Secret mounted as a file still weaker than a secret manager?", "When etcd or RBAC lets others read it.", "When the file mode inside the pod is 0644.", "When the Secret is larger than one MiB.", "When the pod mounts it rather than env vars.", "Encoding is not encryption. Prefer a manager and a short lease. If you use a Secret, turn on encryption at rest and tight RBAC."),
    q("sec-secrets", "easy", "Where must a production password not live?", "In a container image.", "In a secret manager.", "In a CI secret store.", "In the process's memory.", "Images, git, and chat get cloned and pulled. Those are the leaks."),
    q("sec-secrets", "hard", "You rotated a key and the old one still works. What did the rotation miss?", "Disabling the old key everywhere.", "Restarting the apps using the key.", "Updating the key in the manager.", "Clearing the manager's key cache.", "Issuing a new key does not revoke the old one. Find every consumer, disable the old credential, and watch who breaks."),
    q("sec-secrets", "medium", "Why do SOPS or Sealed Secrets belong in a GitOps repo?", "Git holds ciphertext, and only the key holder decrypts.", "Git holds plaintext, and the cluster encrypts on apply.", "Git holds a reference, and the cluster fetches the value.", "Git holds a hash, and reviewers compare it to the value.", "Review that a secret changed. Do not review the secret. Fetching by reference is the External Secrets pattern."),
    q("sec-pki", "medium", "Of a certificate and its private key, which can you hand to a client?", "The certificate.", "The private key.", "Both of them.", "Neither of them.", "The certificate is public and names the key. The key proves you hold it. Giving away the key lets someone be you."),
    q("sec-pki", "medium", "The API server certificate expired and kubectl fails, but workloads keep serving. Why can both be true?", "App traffic does not pass through the API server.", "The kubelet caches the cert for 24 hours.", "Pods use their own copy of the API server cert.", "kube-proxy re-signs the cert for the pods.", "Clients of the API check that certificate. The data plane keeps serving traffic. Renew the control plane certs and restart the static pods."),
    q("sec-pki", "easy", "When does a client trust a certificate from a private CA?", "When the CA is in its trust store.", "When the key is 4096 bits or more.", "When the certificate is self-signed.", "When the hostname is in the subject.", "A private CA is not public trust. Install it where the clients are. Matching the name is the other half."),
    q("sec-pki", "hard", "mTLS between services fails after a rotation, while the server cert still verifies. What else rotated badly?", "The client cert, or the server's client CA bundle.", "The server's private key, which no longer matches.", "The intermediate on the server's certificate.", "The cipher suites the server offers clients.", "Both directions have a trust store. Rotate them as a pair and keep a grace period. A bad server key or chain would fail server verification."),
    q("sec-image", "medium", "Trivy reported a critical CVE in a base image. What is the fix?", "Rebuild on a patched base, then redeploy.", "Add the CVE to the scanner's ignore file.", "Restart the pods so they pull the base again.", "Pin the base image by digest to freeze it.", "The scanner does not patch. The release is a new digest. Pinning keeps the vulnerable bytes in place."),
    q("sec-image", "easy", "Why does a smaller base image help?", "Fewer packages, so fewer CVEs and tools.", "Smaller images are not scanned for CVEs.", "Smaller images run with fewer syscalls.", "Smaller images cannot run a shell at all.", "Still scan it. Less is not none, and an attacker who lands has less to work with."),
    q("sec-image", "medium", "You scan in CI, and production runs a different tag. What did the scan buy you?", "Little, because you scanned other bytes.", "A baseline that covers later tags.", "Coverage, as long as the base matches.", "Coverage, if the tags share a prefix.", "Scan the digest you run, or admit only digests you have scanned."),
    q("sec-image", "hard", "Why keep both a dependency scanner and an image scanner?", "They see different package types.", "They use different CVE databases.", "One scans at build, one at runtime.", "One finds CVEs, the other licenses.", "One reads lockfiles and app packages. The other reads OS package databases in the image. Neither sees both reliably. Run both on the artifact you ship."),
    q("sec-sbom", "medium", "Why is an SBOM useful the morning a new CVE is published?", "You can search what you shipped for it.", "It lists which CVEs you are exposed to.", "It shows which services call the library.", "It tells you the patched version to use.", "Generate it from the build and store it with the digest. A scanner maps it to CVEs."),
    q("sec-sbom", "medium", "What does checking cosign signatures at admission mean?", "Only images signed by a trusted key can run.", "Only images with no critical CVEs can run.", "Only images from a private registry can run.", "Only images rebuilt this week can run.", "Verify at the cluster. A private registry still accepts whatever a stolen push credential uploads, and a signature says nothing about CVEs."),
    q("sec-sbom", "easy", "Why is signing a digest stronger than signing a tag?", "A tag can later point at other bytes.", "A digest signature also covers the tag.", "A tag cannot hold a signature at all.", "A digest signature expires less often.", "The digest is the bytes. Sign the digest, and point a tag at it if humans need a name."),
    q("sec-sbom", "hard", "An SBOM lists a library and the scanner did not flag it. What do you trust for 'are we running it'?", "An SBOM made from the image you run.", "The newest SBOM in the artifact store.", "The scanner's report of the image.", "The lockfile in the release's git tag.", "SBOMs drift when they are not produced from the shipped artifact. Regenerate from that digest."),
    q("sec-pipeline", "medium", "What does building and deploying with the same cloud admin key mean?", "Any code in the build can deploy.", "Builds are reproducible across runs.", "The key is safe once it is masked.", "Deploys need the build to pass first.", "A dependency's install script runs with the same power. Separate build identity from deploy identity."),
    q("sec-pipeline", "hard", "What is pull_request_target plus a checkout of the PR head plus secrets?", "Untrusted code running with secrets.", "Safe, since the workflow file is trusted.", "Safe, if the token is read-only.", "Safe, if the PR is from the same org.", "The workflow file is trusted, but the code it checks out is not. Run untrusted code with pull_request and no secrets."),
    q("sec-pipeline", "easy", "What are protected environments in a CI system for?", "Gating production credentials behind approval.", "Running jobs on isolated, dedicated runners.", "Storing production secrets encrypted at rest.", "Blocking deploys outside business hours.", "The production job should be harder to start than the unit tests. Isolation and encryption are separate controls."),
    q("sec-pipeline", "medium", "A CI job runs docker build using the host daemon's socket. What did the job get?", "Root-level control of the build host.", "Access limited to building images.", "A rootless runtime, scoped to the job.", "Read-only access to the host's images.", "If the daemon is rootful, the socket is the host. Prefer a rootless or isolated builder."),
    q("sec-runtime", "medium", "Why have Falco or Tetragon in the stack?", "To detect suspicious behavior in running workloads.", "To block unsigned images at admission time, per namespace.", "To scan images for CVEs in the registry.", "To enforce NetworkPolicy at the node level.", "Runtime tools see what got through. Policy should have stopped the easy cases earlier."),
    q("sec-runtime", "easy", "How does a RuntimeDefault seccomp profile compare with Unconfined?", "It blocks risky syscalls the runtime picks.", "It allows only the syscalls the app used.", "It logs syscalls but blocks none of them.", "It drops all Linux capabilities as well.", "Start from the runtime default. Add a custom profile only when you have measured the syscalls the app needs."),
    q("sec-runtime", "hard", "A seccomp profile breaks the app on one syscall. What repair keeps the lockdown?", "Allow that one syscall after confirming it.", "Switch the pod to RuntimeDefault for now.", "Set the profile's default action to log.", "Grant the capability the syscall checks.", "The profile is a list. Open the one hole you can justify. A log default turns the profile off."),
    q("sec-runtime", "medium", "Why does dropping all capabilities break an app that binds port 80?", "Ports below 1024 need NET_BIND_SERVICE.", "Ports below 1024 need CAP_NET_ADMIN.", "Ports below 1024 need SYS_ADMIN.", "Ports below 1024 need NET_RAW.", "Add the one capability, or bind a high port and let the proxy hold 80. Do not hand out SYS_ADMIN for one low port."),
    q("sec-audit", "medium", "Which question do Kubernetes audit logs answer?", "Who called the API, and what they did.", "Which pods talked to each other.", "Which processes ran in a container.", "Which requests the app served.", "If audit logging is off, an incident has no actor. Turn it on before you need it."),
    q("sec-audit", "easy", "How does Pod Security audit mode differ from enforce?", "Audit records it. Enforce rejects it.", "Audit rejects it. Enforce records it.", "Audit warns the user. Enforce logs it.", "Audit checks pods. Enforce checks nodes.", "Use audit and warn to see what would break before you enforce."),
    q("sec-audit", "medium", "What is CloudTrail or the cloud audit log the source for?", "Who made control-plane API calls.", "What data was read from a bucket.", "Which packets crossed the VPC.", "What ran inside an instance.", "Data reads need data events, packets need flow logs, and processes need host telemetry. Keep each for its own question."),
    q("sec-audit", "hard", "An attacker with API access set the audit policy to None. Why is a remote copy the real control?", "Logs inside the cluster can be deleted by the attacker.", "Remote copies record calls the API server skips.", "The audit policy only applies to remote backends.", "Local logs are rotated before anyone can read them.", "Ship the audit stream somewhere the admin of the compromised cluster cannot delete."),
    q("sec-incident", "medium", "A node is suspected of a container escape. What do you do before reimaging it?", "Cordon, isolate, and snapshot it.", "Drain it to move pods off quickly.", "Reboot it to kill the attacker.", "Upgrade its kernel in place.", "Contain first. Decide explicitly if you are preserving evidence. Draining moves possibly compromised pods elsewhere, and a reboot destroys memory evidence."),
    q("sec-incident", "easy", "When do you revoke cloud keys found in a leaked repo?", "Immediately.", "After the history rewrite.", "After the postmortem.", "If the logs show use.", "The key is live. History rewrite does not disable it, and the absence of use in logs is not proof."),
    q("sec-incident", "hard", "A workload identity that can read a bucket is compromised. What is containment?", "Revoke its sessions and see what it read.", "Delete the pod so the identity is dropped.", "Rotate the bucket's encryption key.", "Move the pod to a new namespace.", "The credential is the identity, not the pod. The role still exists for the next pod, and stolen sessions stay valid until revoked."),
    q("sec-incident", "medium", "What should a NetworkPolicy that isolates a namespace during an incident do?", "Cut unexpected flows but keep admin access.", "Block all ingress and egress, with no exceptions.", "Allow only traffic from the monitoring stack.", "Block egress, and leave ingress as it was.", "Isolation is a change. Make it one you can undo, and do not lock yourself out."),
    q("sec-zero", "medium", "What does zero trust mean for a platform?", "Network location grants no access.", "Every packet is inspected deeply.", "No traffic leaves the private network.", "Every user is re-verified each hour.", "Each call authenticates and is allowed on purpose. Identity, mTLS, and policy sit on top of a network that is no longer the only control."),
    q("sec-zero", "easy", "Why is a 'trusted internal subnet' a weak boundary?", "One compromised host reaches everything.", "Internal subnets cannot use encryption.", "Internal subnets skip the firewall rules.", "Internal subnets cannot be audited.", "Assume the neighbor is hostile. Authenticate the call."),
    q("sec-zero", "medium", "Why are short-lived workload certificates better than a long-lived token?", "A stolen one soon expires.", "They cannot be copied off.", "They need no authorization.", "They do not need a CA to issue.", "Pair a short lifetime with an issuer you can shut off."),
    q("sec-zero", "hard", "You terminated TLS at the edge and called the rest of the cluster trusted. What is missing?", "Authentication between services.", "Encryption from the client to edge.", "A certificate from a public CA.", "A WAF in front of the ingress.", "Anything already inside can call anything. mTLS or an equivalent identity on service calls is the inside half."),
    q("sec-threat", "medium", "Before choosing a scanner, what does a threat model for a cluster ask?", "Assets, attackers, and trust boundaries.", "Which compliance frameworks apply.", "Which CVEs are in the current images.", "Which tools the team already knows.", "Tools come after the abuse case. A scanner does not know what you are afraid of."),
    q("sec-threat", "easy", "What is a trust boundary in a platform?", "Where the caller's privilege changes.", "Where traffic leaves the cloud region.", "Where one team's ownership ends.", "Where encryption is first applied.", "The edge, the node, and the API server are examples. Draw the boundaries. The attacks you care about cross one."),
    q("sec-threat", "hard", "When does a container escape matter more than a bug in one app?", "When other pods share the node.", "When the app runs as a non-root user.", "When the pod has no NetworkPolicy.", "When the app holds no sensitive data.", "Shared nodes share fate. The escape becomes every other pod on it, plus the node role."),
    q("sec-threat", "medium", "You modeled a stolen CI token. What is the useful control?", "Scope it to one environment and expire it.", "Store it encrypted in the CI system.", "Mask it in every log the CI writes.", "Rotate it on a fixed schedule, once a year.", "Model the token's power, then reduce it. Encryption and masking help keep it from leaking but do nothing once it is stolen."),
    q("sec-vault", "medium", "An OpenBao or Vault server just booted and will not serve secrets. What state is it in?", "Sealed", "Standby", "Uninitialized", "Recovery", "Unseal is the step between process-up and able-to-read. It needs key shares or auto-unseal. A standby forwards requests to the active node."),
    q("sec-vault", "hard", "What does transit auto-unseal mean?", "Another Vault decrypts the root key at boot.", "Each node holds a share and unseals the others.", "The unseal keys are stored in a cloud KMS.", "The root token unseals it during startup.", "Operators no longer type key shares at boot. You still protect the transit token. You moved the unseal problem, not deleted it. A cloud KMS is a different seal type."),
    q("sec-vault", "easy", "What should an application's token policy allow?", "Only the paths that app needs.", "Read on all paths, write on its own.", "All paths, with a short token TTL.", "The same paths as the root token.", "Issue a token or an AppRole for the workload. Keep root for recovery."),
    q("sec-vault", "medium", "Why are Vault's dynamic database credentials better than a static password?", "They expire and are tied to the client.", "They are stronger, with longer passwords.", "They skip the database's own auth.", "They are cached on each client host.", "When the lease ends, the grant ends. A static password lives until someone remembers it."),
    q("sec-bench", "medium", "What is kube-bench?", "A CIS benchmark check of cluster config.", "A load test for the Kubernetes API.", "A runtime detector for node attacks.", "A scanner for vulnerable cluster images.", "Run it to see the control plane flags and file permissions you drifted from. Fix the findings you accept."),
    q("sec-bench", "easy", "What is Lynis for on a Linux host?", "Auditing its hardening.", "Scanning its open ports.", "Detecting live intrusions.", "Patching its packages.", "It reports sshd, permission and kernel weaknesses on the box. It does not watch a running attack."),
    q("sec-bench", "medium", "What does osquery let you do?", "Query live host state with SQL.", "Collect host logs into one store.", "Block processes by policy rules.", "Scan hosts for unpatched CVEs.", "Processes, sockets, and files become a query you can repeat across the fleet."),
    q("sec-bench", "hard", "What does running an InSpec compliance profile in CI mean?", "Drift from the hardening rules fails the build.", "The profile hardens each image as it builds.", "The rules produce a report for the auditor.", "The profile tests the app for exploitable bugs.", "Encode the rule you care about so the next image cannot quietly drop it."),
    q("sec-cspm", "medium", "A cloud posture tool flags a public bucket and an open security group. What will it not tell you?", "That a container just spawned a shell.", "That an IAM user has no MFA enabled.", "That a database snapshot is public.", "That CloudTrail is turned off.", "CSPM looks at cloud configuration. Pair it with runtime and identity, or you only see the standing configuration."),
    q("sec-cspm", "easy", "How do you fix the first public-bucket finding?", "Block public access and verify anonymously.", "Make the objects private, one at a time.", "Mark it as accepted risk in the tool.", "Rename the bucket so it is not guessable.", "Confirm with an anonymous request. The console flag and the bytes on the internet should agree."),
    q("sec-cspm", "medium", "What is an unused admin access key older than a year?", "A standing credential to disable.", "Low risk, since it is not in use.", "Safe, if MFA is on for the user.", "Fine, if it is stored encrypted.", "Old keys are how a laptop you recycled still opens the account. MFA does not apply to access key calls."),
    q("sec-cspm", "hard", "The posture scan is green and an attacker is in a pod. How is that consistent?", "Posture checks configuration, not activity.", "The scan ran before the attacker got in.", "The pod's namespace was excluded from the scan.", "Posture tools skip pods with service meshes.", "The cloud configuration can be tidy while a workload is compromised. Use posture for standing mistakes and runtime detection for the thing happening now."),
]
